# Target B: Type-II atomic-gap transfer into Section 39

Date: 2026-10-03
Base main: 6276af2c645b4fe31c56ce9bb25b43675f5c8b99
Status: proof-only cross-route corollary, noncanonical. Target B, C-II, and Level I remain open.

This note applies only to sequences that satisfy both the pure-rhombic-disk jammed
hypotheses of Target B Section 39 and the audited-working Type-II train-measure
hypotheses of Target C Sections 142/168. It does not extend Section 168 to every
Target B pure-rhombic disk.

Let

    x = lim T/D,
    y = lim q/T,

along a common subsequence, and let sigma be the total atomic mass of the limiting
projective train-direction measure. Put

    Delta_2 = pi/(2 sqrt(3)),
    s_2 = sqrt(Delta_2),
    A = 2 s_2 + 0.0004.

Target B Section 39 gives

    sigma <= 1-y/2                                      (1)

and, from pinned rigidity,

    y >= 1/2.                                           (2)

Audited Target C Section 168 gives in its Type-II scope

    x sigma >= A.                                       (3)

Combining (1) and (3),

    A <= x(1-y/2),

so

    x >= A/(1-y/2).                                     (4)

Using (2),

    boxed{
    liminf T/D >= (4/3)A
                  = (8/3)sqrt(Delta_2) + 1/1875
                  = 2.540034151637219... .
    }                                                   (5)

Since sqrt(Delta_2)=1/sqrt(c), c=2sqrt(3)/pi, this improves the Section-39 endpoint

    8/(3sqrt(c)) = 2.539500818303886...

by the explicit amount

    1/1875 = 0.000533333333333... .

This is a uniform asymptotic gap on the common scope, not merely endpoint
nonattainment.

Section 39 also has q <= pi D + o(D), hence xy <= pi. Combining that with (4) gives

    boxed{
    limsup q/T <= pi/(A+pi/2)
                 = 0.903841654534597... .
    }                                                   (6)

Finally,

    (T-q)/D = x(1-y),

and A(1-y)/(1-y/2) is decreasing in y. Substituting the upper bound in (6) gives

    boxed{
    liminf (T-q)/D >= A-pi/2
                     = 0.334229286933018... .
    }                                                   (7)

Because B=2T in the pure-rhombic disk,

    liminf B/D >= 16/(3sqrt(c)) + 2/1875.               (8)

The new step is only this cross-route transfer. Equation (3) is the already audited
finite-diagonal atomic Mantel theorem; no atomic-mass semicontinuity argument is added
here. No numerical search, verifier execution, or CI result is used as a proof premise.
The decimals only evaluate the closed forms.

The result still does not imply the Target-B shell inequality G+E >= (sqrt(3)/4)B,
and it does not close C-II. The remaining bottleneck is a physical conversion of the
strict train/atomic gap into shell, penetration, boundary-turning, or source-level
competitor currency.
